      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The idea that privacy is a luxury we can only afford when the state is safe is a dangerous fallacy that excuses tyranny under the guise of necessity. A government that fails to protect citizens from digital threats has indeed failed its duty, but that failure does not grant it the license to destroy the very liberties that make a free society possible. True security requires a state that respects individual rights, not one that sacrifices them to maintain power, and therefore the principle of privacy remains absolute regardless of the state's current competence.",
      "source": "target",
